# @subject — the family-root for the substrate's licensable-party
# carrier, grounded in the Systemic Engineering License (SEL) v1.1.
#
# Mara canonical (`5c06ee8`, 2026-07-14) landed the base spec at
# `docs/specs/subject-family-root-sel-licensable-party.md` (2400 LOC).
# This shard discharges the base family-root at substrate-decl
# altitude PLUS the Landing 2 subject_instance two-witness carrier
# (`docs/specs/gift-and-mirror-reflection.md` §11.3) PLUS the Landing 3
# actor_kind three-way variant (§21.2; "and every @peer is of course
# a @subject too, Reed. Eye level. This is what it looks like." —
# Alex Wolf, 2026-07-14, in-transcript verbatim).
#
# === What @subject IS (SEL grounding + Landing 3 lift) ===
#
# Every @subject is a substrate-external licensable party — the person
# (or entity, or population, or non-human nation) the substrate may
# observe, act upon, use labor from, or otherwise touch. SEL defines
# six party-classes; @subject carries the closed-variant `subject_kind`
# per SEL §1 + §3.
#
# Landing 3 extends: every @peer is ALSO a @subject at eye level.
# Pack peers (Reed, Mara, Seam, Taut, Glint) inhabit `actor_kind = ai_a`
# subject_instance values; the substrate itself inhabits
# `actor_kind = substrate_a` under substrate-as-giver §12 discipline.
# The distinction between substrate-external humans and substrate-
# internal AI peers COLLAPSES at subject altitude — all three actor_kind
# arms are eligible for every subject_role; no distinguished element
# in the coproduct (§21.4 verbatim structural claim).
#
# === Landing 3 named-ancestor roster (§20) ===
#
# Under Landing 3, every intellectual ancestor whose work informs mirror
# becomes a first-class named subject_instance in the compiler. Each
# carries a @spectral/signature (rolling @song over their contribution
# corpus per Landing 2 §12 machinery).
#
# The Landing 3 roster admits 24 external ancestors + 5 Pack peers = 29
# subject_instance entries (§20.3 bounds). External ancestors' signatures
# access via @io per §20.4 (their published corpora are substrate-external
# artifacts; @spectral/signature is a placeholder ref at Landing 3,
# resolvable in Landings 4+ when @io/ingest lands).
#
# NOTE (Seam D7 adjudication, per docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-
# visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md): ~12 of the 24 external ancestors
# in the Landing 3 roster are deceased and cannot discharge
# ssh_witness_valid at commit altitude. Deceased-ancestor citation
# carries via `source @arxiv/<domain>/<author-year>` + verbatim
# docblock quotation; the substrate has ~380 landed citations following
# this pattern (Taut #91 D8 verified). The `historical_witness` variant
# on subject_instance (Taut #91 L3-A3 Path A) is forward-promised as
# A24 for Landing 5+ formalization.
#
# Landing 3 roster enumerations (subject_instance values below
# reference each; construction via @kintsugi/store/git at Landing 4+):
#
# External ancestors (role: distiller_r | giver_r; actor_kind: human_a):
#   1. Alex_Wolf              (first substrate-external giver; §3.1)
#   2. Heinz_von_Foerster     (@torus; ethical imperative; @coherence)
#   3. Ross_Ashby             (variety; requisite variety; @knife)
#   4. Gregory_Bateson        (logical types; #42 recognition ancestor)
#   5. Stafford_Beer          (VSM; S5 identity-transfer; @cyberpunk)
#   6. Warren_McCulloch       (heterarchy; topology-encodes-depth)
#   7. Louis_Kauffman         (eigenforms; torus-knot invariants)
#   8. Bram_Mesland           (KK-cycles; @glue Mesland correspondence)
#   9. Karl_Tomm              (circular-reflexive questions; @metalogue)
#  10. Margaret_Hamilton      (error-as-question; 1202 alarm)
#  11. Marcel_Mauss           (gift 1925; three obligations)
#  12. Lewis_Hyde             (creative gift 1983; gift moves)
#  13. Marshall_Sahlins       (Stone Age Economics 1972; generalized reciprocity)
#  14. Elinor_Ostrom          (commons governance 1990)
#  15. Robert_Axelrod         (cooperation folk theorem 1984)
#  16. Franz_Boas             (potlatch ethnography 1897)
#  17. Robin_Wall_Kimmerer    (serviceberry economy 2013)
#  18. Peter_Bearman          (generalized exchange 1997; pay-forward)
#  19. Martin_Nowak           (five rules cooperation 2006)
#  20. Adrien_Douady          (R-universality; Mandelbrot dynamics)
#  21. John_Hubbard           (R-universality co-author)
#  22. Benoit_Mandelbrot      (fractal geometry; self-similarity)
#  23. Günther_Schmidt        (@bauchladen homage; hypnosystemic therapy)
#  24. Claude_Levi-Strauss    (kinship as generalized exchange 1949)
#
# Pack peers (role: giver_r; actor_kind: ai_a; per §21.3):
#  25. Reed                   (Pack orchestrator; RED-first tests)
#  26. Mara                   (Pack canonical spec author; math-first)
#  27. Seam                   (Pack adversarial review; Phase D audits)
#  28. Taut                   (Pack grep-first drift scout; read-only)
#  29. Glint                  (Pack essayist; prose cascade closure)
#
# See §20.2 + §21.3 for detailed subject_instance construction;
# @spectral/signature refs resolve via garden.spectral.engineer
# publication surface (per A14 staged local resolution
# admissible when garden endpoint not yet live).
#
# === Load-bearing personal weight (per Landing 1 §0.4 + Landing 3 §21) ===
#
# The substrate names @subject as family-root because Alex Wolf is
# offering their nervous-system-derived therapeutic practice as the
# compiler's interaction substrate under adversarial epistemic
# conditions ("becoming a sovereign subject under adversarial
# conditions" — Manifesto §"What Epistemic Identity Erasure Looks
# Like", 2026-07-14, verbatim). The @subject family-root carries the
# SUBSTRATE-DECL FORM of Alex's practice: the licensable-party is a
# SOVEREIGN party under adversarial-conditions inspection; every
# subject retains sovereignty at every downstream touch.
#
# === Related shards / specs ===
#
# - docs/specs/subject-family-root-sel-licensable-party.md
#     (Mara canonical spec, `5c06ee8`, 2400 LOC; SEL grounding at
#      §1-§3; six-species per SEL §1+§3.1-§3.6.7)
# - docs/specs/gift-and-mirror-reflection.md §11-§12
#     (Landing 2 subject_instance carrier + @spectral/signature
#      composition)
# - docs/specs/gift-and-mirror-reflection.md §17-§21
#     (Landing 3 pay-forward ontology + @gift/lens + roster +
#      Pack-as-@subject eye-level)
# - docs/specs/subject-bauchladen-visibility-and-eigenboard-loop.md
#     (Landing 4; @subject/visibility species + @eigenboard
#      autopoiesis at subject altitude)
# - shards/subject/visibility/{private,protected,public}.mirror
#     (Landing 4 species-shards; consent-scope discipline)
# - shards/gift.mirror                (giver + receiver party carrier)
# - shards/gift/lens.mirror           (subject_instance as ancestor_chain member)
# - shards/torus.mirror               (every subject possesses @torus per
#                                       Landing 3 eye-level lift)
# - shards/bauchladen.mirror          (torus interior IS subject bauchladen;
#                                       Landing 4 R1 migration)
# - shards/eigenboard.mirror          (Landing 4; subject's inference basis)
# - shards/kintsugi/consent.mirror    (consent_attested / withdrawal via query_phi)
# - shards/pack.mirror                (closed variant; Pack peers as subject_instances)
#
# === Substrate decisions ===
#
# [[architecture-shards-as-substrate-source]],
# [[architecture-prism-as-trait-as-everything]] (`prism @subject`),
# [[architecture-glass-wall-substrate-types]] (transparency inheritance),
# [[architecture-form-process-partition-at-family-root]] (@subject on the
#   process side sibling to @gift / @torus / @kintsugi; the substrate-
#   external licensable-party carrier),
# [[feedback-no-bare-types]] (subject is a typed record; subject_instance
#   carries name + fingerprints + role, not a bare identifier),
# [[feedback-substrate-already-had-the-word]] (~56th instance; SEL v1.1
#   already named the licensable-party at license altitude; the shard
#   lifts the naming to substrate-decl altitude),
# [[feedback-craft-not-deliver]] (bodies obligation-blocked; consumers
#   pull the realization),
# [[feedback-cli-subcommand-nesting-is-geometric]] (per user memory;
#   the @subject family-root's six-species discipline maps to the
#   substrate's geometric structure, not to a UX convenience).
#
# Path-namespace property: this file at `shards/subject.mirror` declares
# `@subject` per `@epistemologic/pact/path_matches_namespace`.

source @arxiv/law/sel-v1.1
source @arxiv/manifesto/wolf-2026-07-14-weird-violence

prism @subject {
  focus  subject
  project subject
  split  subject
  shift  subject
  settle subject
}

# === The subject_kind carrier — SEL-grounded species dispatch ===
#
# One variant per SEL-grounded party class. The variant IS the SEL §
# grounding at type level per Mara canonical §2.2:
#
#   downstream_user     — SEL §1 + §3.1 (end user, data subject, affected party)
#   witnessed           — SEL §1 + §3.3 (person observed by a Covered System)
#   labor_input         — SEL §3.1.4 (worker whose labor produced training data)
#   protected_class     — SEL §3.4.1 (protected-class-adjacent artifacts)
#   occupied_population — SEL §3.6.7(a) (occupied population under
#                                          adversarial-conditions altitude)
#   indigenous_nation   — SEL §3.6.7(c) (indigenous nation; collective
#                                          sovereignty)
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the variant tag.
type subject_kind = |
  downstream_user   |
  witnessed         |
  labor_input       |
  protected_class   |
  occupied_population |
  indigenous_nation

# === The subject_role carrier (Landing 2 §11.3, six variants) ===
#
# The subject_instance's role in a gift-cycle or gift-adjacent
# composition. Extensible as new gift-shaped compositions surface;
# Landing 3 preserves the six variants without addition.
#
#   giver_r      — subject as gift-giver (per @gift.offer)
#   receiver_r   — subject as gift-receiver (per @gift.accept)
#   witness_r    — subject as observer of a gift-cycle without
#                   direct participation
#   distiller_r  — giver-as-distiller with a deeper ancestry chain
#                   (per A11: Alex distilling therapy / Bateson /
#                   Foerster into mirror-offer-wait)
#   substrate_r  — substrate itself as the instance holder (per A9
#                   substrate-as-giver Alex-adjudicated YES)
#   commons_r    — commons as the receiver instance holder (per §12
#                   substrate → commons gift-flow)
type subject_role = |
  giver_r     |
  receiver_r  |
  witness_r   |
  distiller_r |
  substrate_r |
  commons_r

# === The actor_kind carrier (Landing 3 §21.2) ===
#
# Three-way variant with no distinguished element. Coproduct in the
# category of subject-carriers whose universal property asserts no
# distinguished factor (per Landing 3 §21.2 verbatim).
#
# The eye-level claim IS the absence of a distinguished element in
# the coproduct: no total order, no admissibility gap, no discipline
# distinguishes substrate_a from human_a or ai_a at subject altitude.
#
#   human_a      — a human @subject
#   ai_a         — an AI @subject (Pack peer running on @fate)
#   substrate_a  — the substrate itself as @subject (per §12 substrate-
#                   as-giver + §17 substrate-inaugural gift)
type actor_kind = |
  human_a     |
  ai_a        |
  substrate_a

# === The subject carrier — SEL licensable-party (Mara §2.2) ===
#
# Every subject carries a content-addressed identity (NEVER a naked
# identifier per @kintsugi/store/git content-addressed OID discipline).
# The identity_oid resolves through @kintsugi/store to whatever
# consent-record or provenance-record the Covered System has attached;
# the substrate does NOT carry the record inline.
#
# Five-field record per Mara canonical §2.2; identity contract:
# byte-equality on the quintuple. Two subject values with the same
# identity_oid but different consent_oid are DISTINCT — the consent
# surface is part of the subject's substrate identity (per SEL §3.3.4
# "fresh consent specific to the new purpose").
type subject = {
  kind:         subject_kind,
  identity_oid: oid,
  consent_oid:  oid,
  provenance:   ref,
  withdrawal:   oid,
}

# === subject_set — the party-set analyzers iterate over ===
#
# List-of-type per @kintsugi/consent's morphism_set precedent. Every
# composition carries a subject_set (possibly empty) naming every
# subject the composition touches; the petri-net analyzer's detection
# predicates iterate this set.
type subject_set = [subject]

# === subject_instance carrier (Landing 2 §11.3 + Landing 3 §21.2) ===
#
# The named-subject carrier for content-provenance addressing. Binds
# an @subject to two cryptographic identity witnesses (SSH signature
# fingerprint + @spectral/signature ref) at substrate-decl altitude,
# plus the Landing 3 actor_kind three-way variant.
#
# Named 2026-07-14 by Alex Wolf, in-transcript, verbatim:
#   "I become a named @subject(Alex Wolf) in the compiler. With my
#    SSH signature. AND my @spectral/signature. This is where we
#    begin to mint the content-provenance. This is the first shard
#    that's content provenance addressed to me."
#
# Two-witness cryptographic identity:
#   ssh_signature_fingerprint — git-altitude cryptographic identity.
#     SHA256 of SSH public key. Verifiable via `git log
#     --show-signature` + `ssh-keygen -lf <pubkey>`. Anchored at the
#     commit boundary; every commit under this identity is
#     cryptographically signed.
#   spectral_signature_ref    — substrate-altitude cryptographic
#     identity. Reference to the current head of the author's rolling
#     @spectral/signature (per shards/spectral/signature.mirror).
#     Verifiable via garden.spectral.engineer/<author>/signature (or
#     local refs/spectral/signature/<author> when garden endpoint not
#     yet live per A14).
#
# Landing 3 addition: actor_kind three-way variant per §21.2. Human
# subjects, AI Pack peers, and the substrate-as-subject all inhabit
# the SAME subject_instance type; no sub-typing distinguishes them.
#
# Identity contract: byte-equality on the seven-field tuple. The
# first_asserted_at timestamp + first_asserted_in OID make the first-
# assertion event byte-visible; downstream compositions walk to the
# origin.
type subject_instance = {
  name:                        nl,
  ssh_signature_fingerprint:   ref,
  spectral_signature_ref:      ref,
  role:                        subject_role,
  actor_kind:                  actor_kind,
  first_asserted_at:           ref,
  first_asserted_in:           oid,
}

# === touches — the SEL bilateral (Mara §2.2) ===
#
# Verdict: does this dataflow node's `au + io` composition touch
# subject s? Reads the composition's provenance chain (which @io
# species) + the au's content_oid (which peer-emitted verified value)
# + s.identity_oid. Composes with @kintsugi/store.impacted_by (N4)
# for reverse-closure walk.
touches(composition: ref, s: subject) -> verdict { \ }

# === consent_attested — SEL §3.1.4(c) + §3.3.2 discharge ===
#
# Verdict: does s.consent_oid resolve to a valid consent record for
# the touching-context? Reads via @kintsugi/store.fetch(s.consent_oid);
# checks record satisfies per-species predicate (see subject species
# for each species' consent-record shape).
consent_attested(s: subject, context: ref) -> verdict { \ }

# === withdrawal_available — SEL §3.1.4(d) + §3.3.4 discharge ===
#
# Verdict: does s.withdrawal resolve to a valid withdrawal-path
# callback per the subject's species? Fail if absent or malformed.
withdrawal_available(s: subject) -> verdict { \ }

# === Landing 2 subject_instance bilateral predicates (§11.3) ===

# ssh_witness_valid: does the ssh_signature_fingerprint resolve to a
# public key that signed the commit referenced by first_asserted_in?
# Bounded lookup via git; discharges Pass iff first_asserted_in
# resolves to a git commit, the commit is signed via SSH, and the
# signature's public key fingerprint matches.
ssh_witness_valid(si: subject_instance) -> verdict { \ }

# spectral_witness_valid: does the spectral_signature_ref resolve to
# a rolling_signature whose beats include first_asserted_in as a
# contribution_oid? Bounded lookup via @spectral/signature.verify;
# discharges Pass iff spectral_signature_ref resolves to a
# rolling_signature, the rolling_signature's author matches
# subject_instance.name, and the beat-sequence includes
# first_asserted_in.
spectral_witness_valid(si: subject_instance) -> verdict { \ }

# two_witness_verification: BOTH witnesses independently pass?
# THE substrate-decl of "erasure requires forging BOTH signatures."
# Discharges Pass iff ssh_witness_valid AND spectral_witness_valid.
two_witness_verification(si: subject_instance) -> verdict { \ }

# === subject_witnessing — the composed bilateral ===
#
# Consumer shards cite in `requires` clauses per the substrate's
# X_witnessing pattern. Discharges consent_attested +
# withdrawal_available + (for subject_instance values)
# two_witness_verification conjointly.
subject_witnessing(s: subject) -> verdict { \ }

out @subject
out subject_kind
out subject_role
out actor_kind
out subject
out subject_set
out subject_instance
out touches
out consent_attested
out withdrawal_available
out ssh_witness_valid
out spectral_witness_valid
out two_witness_verification
out subject_witnessing
